<div>
<h2 id="news-hook">The Popular Kindle Libby Workaround Is Finally Broken</h2>

<p>For more than a decade, US Kindle owners have used the same quiet workaround to hang onto library e-books: borrow a Libby or OverDrive title, download it to the Kindle, flip the device into airplane mode, and keep reading even after the loan's due date passes. Because the offline Kindle never sees Amazon's return notice, the book stayed put until the reader switched airplane mode off. On August 19-20, 2026, readers around the web reported that this trick no longer works. <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/kindle/airplane-mode-on-the-kindle-wont-extend-libby-e-books-anymore" rel="noopener">A recent Amazon Kindle software update appears to have blocked it</a>, and dozens of users on Reddit report that their borrowed library books now expire on schedule even when the device is in airplane mode.</p>

<p>The change has been arriving through the regular Kindle firmware rollout, and it affects a specific set of devices. Before you panic, know this: the workaround is gone, but reading library books on a Kindle is still completely possible — you just need to follow the loan rules, or use one of the offline-friendly EPUB approaches below. Here is exactly what changed, which devices are affected, and what Kindle readers can do instead.</p>

<h2 id="the-trick">What the Airplane Mode Trick Was</h2>

<p>To understand what changed, it helps to recall how borrowing library e-books on a Kindle works. In the United States, you can connect your Libby or OverDrive account to your Amazon account and send an eligible library loan straight to your Kindle. The book is delivered as a Kindle format file that is tied to your device, and the license is set to expire when your library loan ends — usually after 14 or 21 days.</p>

<p>The long-running trick was simple: download the borrowed book while online, then switch the Kindle to airplane mode before the return date. With no network connection, the Kindle could not retrieve the return notice from Amazon, so the book stayed readable past its due date. Readers who travel, commute, or simply run long on a favorite title leaned on this hack to avoid renewing or waiting in a hold queue again.</p>

<p>You can see this workaround and the full range of ways to read <a href="/blog/libby-library-ebooks-ereader-2026/">Libby library books on any e-reader</a> in our earlier guide. That article also covers which methods are safest — and which have always been fragile.</p>

<h2 id="what-changed">What the Kindle Update Changed</h2>

<p>According to Good e-Reader's report, published August 20, Amazon's latest firmware update makes the affected Kindles reconnect to Amazon and receive the return signal even while airplane mode is on, so borrowed library books now expire on schedule. The outlet spoke to long-time e-reader observers and noted that <a href="https://help.overdrive.com/en-us/" rel="noopener">Amazon and OverDrive have not issued an official announcement</a> about the fix, but the behavior change is being reported across multiple reader communities.</p>

<p>This is not Amazon's first attempt to close the loophole — the two companies have reportedly been trying for years — but earlier efforts never fully stuck. This firmware change appears to be the first that reliably stops the trick on current hardware.</p>

<h2 id="affected-devices">Affected Devices and Firmware Versions</h2>

<p>Not every Kindle is affected, which matters if you are deciding whether to read on an older device or a new one. Based on the early reporting, the airplanes-mode trick now fails on:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Kindle Paperwhite (11th generation)</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Kindle Paperwhite (12th generation)</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Kindle Colorsoft Signature Edition</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Any Kindle running firmware 5.19.5</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>Meanwhile, <strong>older Kindles running firmware 5.19.2 and below mostly still work</strong> for now. That is a useful detail if you keep an older device around, but it is reasonable to assume Amazon will roll this behavior out to remaining models and firmware versions over time, so do not treat an older device as a permanent fix.</p>

<p>Because the rollout is gradual, you may not see the change on every Kindle at once. If your loan expires on schedule for the first time in years, the odds are high that your device has picked up the update.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why This Matters for EPUB Readers</h2>

<p>If you read mostly EPUB files that you own outright, this change barely affects you — your sideloaded EPUB library is offline and independent of any loan window, delivery scheme, or DRM server. The people hit hardest are readers who lean on the library-loan loophole for constant free access.</p>

<p>The deeper lesson is one our site returns to often: <strong>a book you only license is fragile</strong>, while <strong>a book you keep as an EPUB file is yours</strong>. Library loans, Kindle Unlimited reads, and DRM-protected purchases are all revocable at the vendor's discretion. An EPUB in your own library, on your own storage, is not — no firmware update can remove it.</p>

<p>That distinction is the practical heart of converting and keeping your reading collection, whether you are moving your library away from a locked-down device or simply archiving titles you care about. We explain the whole process in our guide to <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">migrating a Kindle library to EPUB</a>.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-do">What to Do Instead: 5 Workable Options</h2>

<p>Losing the airplane mode hack is annoying, but there are several solid alternatives depending on how you read. Here are the approaches that still work in 2026.</p>

<h3 id="opt1">1. Follow the Loan Window and Use Renewals</h3>

<p>The most reliable path is to read within your loan period and renew when you need more time. Libby lets you renew a loan early as long as no one else is waiting on the title. If nobody has a hold, you can often chain renewals and keep a book almost indefinitely without any trickery. Check the book's page in the Libby app for the renew button.</p>

<h3 id="opt2">2. Read Library Books on a Device With Native OverDrive</h3>

<p>Kobo and PocketBook readers have built-in OverDrive and library support that follow the loan window automatically. They do not need airplane mode because the reading experience is designed around loans — and on Kobo, a borrowed book behaves like a normal book until the return date. If you read a lot of library e-books and want an experience without workarounds, <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">comparing Kobo versus Kindle versus PocketBook</a> is a good place to start.</p>

<h3 id="opt3">3. Keep Reading Offline in the Libby App</h3>

<p>You do not need a Kindle at all. The Libby app itself lets you download a borrowed title for offline reading on your phone or tablet. The loan still expires, but the download is already on your device, so for catching up on a few chapters offline it works well without any airplane mode gymnastics.</p>

<h3 id="opt4">4. Own the Book as an EPUB to Keep It Permanently</h3>

<p>For books you genuinely want to keep — the ones you will re-read or reference — the cleanest long-term answer is to own them as EPUB files. If you already own an EPUB version of a title (from a DRM-free store, a direct publisher purchase, or your own files), you can transfer it to a Kindle-compatible format and read it forever with no loan window at all. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">sending EPUB files to your Kindle</a> walks through the delivery process.</p>

<p>Readers coming from Amazon can also move the books they own into a free, portable EPUB library first. See our full walkthrough on <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">converting your Kindle collection to EPUB</a> so no future firmware change can take your titles away.</p>

<h3 id="opt5">5. Consider a DRM-Free Source Going Forward</h3>

<p>As you rebuild or grow your library, favoring DRM-free EPUB sources reduces dependence on any single vendor's servers. When you buy a DRM-free EPUB, the file is yours to store, back up, convert, and read on any device. Our collection of <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB sources</a> lists reputable stores and download sites worth knowing.</p>

<h2 id="the-larger-picture">The Bigger Picture: Loans Are Temporary, Files Are Yours</h2>

<p>The end of the airplane mode trick is a small but revealing moment in the e-book ecosystem. It is a reminder that library loans, subscription reads, and DRM-bound purchases are always at the mercy of the service you are using, and that protections can change without notice — legally and completely.</p>

<p>The most resilient reading setup does not depend on any one of those. It is built on EPUB files you control: backed up in a folder, convertible to whatever format your current device needs, and readable today, next year, and twenty years from now. When a workaround disappears, that library is the only thing that never expires.</p>

<p>If you are affected by the Kindle change and want a permanent way to keep reading on the device you have, start by moving the titles you own into EPUB, then send them back to your Kindle from your own library. You will lose nothing, and no future update will take your books away.</p>

